\begin{abstract}
We develop, in complete and self-contained form, the structural dictionary between
binary self-dual error-correcting codes and the unimodular intersection forms of
smooth closed four-manifolds, and we establish that the dictionary respects the
natural ``gluing'' operations on both sides. On the topology side the gluing
operation is the \emph{orthogonal direct sum} of intersection forms---the algebraic
model of the connected sum $M \# N$ of four-manifolds---under which unimodularity
(Poincar\'e duality), evenness (the spin condition), and standard diagonalizability
are each closed. On the coding side the corresponding operation is \emph{coordinate
concatenation} $C \oplus D$, under which we prove that Hamming weight is additive, the
binary inner product is block-diagonal, cardinality is multiplicative, double-evenness
is preserved, self-orthogonality is preserved, and---the headline closure
result---\emph{self-duality is preserved}. We recall the two length-divisibility
theorems making the bridge sharp: every self-dual doubly-even binary code has length
divisible by $4$ (via the all-ones ``global section'' argument) and in fact by $8$
(Gleason's theorem, via a Gauss-sum / MacWilliams evaluation yielding the master
identity $|C| = (1+i)^n$). These mirror the rank-$8$ divisibility of positive-definite
even unimodular lattices, the regime in which $E_8$---and hence its mod-$2$ shadow, the
extended Hamming $[8,4,4]$ code---lives. As capstone we construct the length-$16$ code
$H_{16} = \mathrm{Hamming} \oplus \mathrm{Hamming}$, the precise mod-$2$ shadow of the
rank-$16$ form $E_8 \oplus E_8$, and derive all of its invariants from the general
closure theorems rather than by brute-force enumeration over $2^{16}$ vectors. All
results have been formally verified.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

The classification of smooth closed simply-connected four-manifolds is controlled to a
remarkable extent by a single algebraic invariant: the \emph{intersection form}, a
symmetric unimodular bilinear form on the second homology lattice. Poincar\'e duality
forces the form to be unimodular; Donaldson's diagonalization theorem forces every
\emph{smooth definite} form to be a standard diagonal of $\pm 1$'s, so that the even
unimodular form $E_8$---algebraically impeccable---describes a topological shape
admitting no smooth structure of the required kind.

In parallel, coding theory has long known that \emph{binary self-dual codes} obey a
structurally identical set of laws: self-duality plays the role of unimodularity,
double-evenness plays the role of the spin/even condition, and the length of a
doubly-even self-dual code is divisible by $8$ (Gleason's theorem), exactly as the rank
of a positive-definite even unimodular lattice is divisible by $8$. The link is not
coincidental: \emph{Construction A} reduces an even unimodular lattice modulo $2$ to a
doubly-even self-dual code, identifying the two divisibility miracles as one.

This paper formalizes the bridge with emphasis on \emph{stability under gluing}. The
natural operation on four-manifolds is the connected sum $M \# N$, whose intersection
form is the orthogonal direct sum of the summands' forms. We prove that the entire
structural package transports to the coding side under coordinate concatenation, and we
deploy the resulting closure theorems to build the length-$16$ code $H_{16}$, the
faithful mod-$2$ image of $E_8 \oplus E_8$. We work over the binary field
$\F_2 = \Z/2\Z$, identifying a binary vector of length $n$ with a function
$\{0,\dots,n-1\} \to \F_2$.

\section{Definitions}

\begin{definition}[Hamming weight]
For a binary vector $v$ of length $n$, $\wt v = |\{ i : v_i = 1 \}|$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Overlap]
$\ovl(x,y) = |\{ i : x_i = 1 \wedge y_i = 1 \}|$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Binary inner product]
$\ip{x}{y} = \sum_i x_i y_i \in \F_2$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Doubly even]
A vector $v$ is \emph{doubly even} when $4 \mid \wt v$; a code $C$ is doubly even when
every $v \in C$ is.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Self-dual code]
A finite set $C \subseteq \F_2^n$ is \emph{self-dual} when it coincides with its own
orthogonal complement: for all $x$, $\;x \in C \iff (\forall y \in C,\ \ip{x}{y} = 0)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[All-ones vector]
$\mathbf{1}_n$ is the constant vector $i \mapsto 1$ of length $n$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Coordinate concatenation]
For $C \subseteq \F_2^m$, $D \subseteq \F_2^n$, the \emph{direct sum} is
\[
  C \oplus D = \{ \append(a,b) : a \in C,\ b \in D \} \subseteq \F_2^{m+n},
\]
where $\append(a,b)$ places the $m$ coordinates of $a$ first and the $n$ coordinates of
$b$ second. We write $\leftPart(z)$ for the first $m$ coordinates of $z$ and
$\rightPart(z)$ for the last $n$, so $\append(\leftPart(z), \rightPart(z)) = z$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Intersection form]
An \emph{intersection form} of rank $n$ is a symmetric integer matrix $G$ (the Gram
matrix). It is \emph{unimodular} when $\det G = \pm 1$; \emph{even} when $v^\top G v$ is
even for all $v$ (equivalently every $G_{ii}$ is even); and \emph{standard
diagonalizable} when $T^\top G T = I$ for some integer change of basis $T$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Orthogonal direct sum of forms]
For forms $Q$ (rank $m$) and $R$ (rank $n$), $Q \oplus R$ has block-diagonal Gram matrix
$\operatorname{diag}(G_Q, G_R)$ reindexed along $\{0,\dots,m-1\} \sqcup \{0,\dots,n-1\}
\cong \{0,\dots,m+n-1\}$. It models the connected sum $M \# N$.
\end{definition}

